    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "This is a geometric series problem. The ball bounces back to a height that is $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the previous height, so this is a sequence with the first term $a = 243$ and common ratio $r = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nThe height of the $n$-th bounce can be given by the formula $h_n = a \\cdot r^{n-1}$. We want to find the smallest $n$ such that $\\left|h_n - 30\\right| < 0$, which is equivalent to $\\left|h_n - 30\\right| < 30$ or $\\left(243 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n-1} - 30\\right) \\cdot \\left(243 \\cdot \\frac{2}{9}\\right)^{n-1} < 0$.\n\nTo qualify for this solution, $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n-1} - \\frac{1}{3} < 0$, which simplifies to $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n-1} < \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nTaking the logarithm base $\\frac{1}{3}$ of both sides yields $2 - (n-1) > 2$, meaning $n > 3$. Therefore, after the $4$-th bounce, the ball reaches a height of less than $30$ cm, and the ball the first rise less than $30$ cm takes $\\boxed{4}$ bounces. The answer is: 4",
